### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants you to concatenate stream elements into a formatted string using `Collectors.joining`. This tests knowledge of the three joining overloads, the requirement for `Stream<CharSequence>`, and efficient string building.

### How do you build a bracketed comma-separated string of names?

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class JoiningDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = Employee.getSampleEmployees();

        String formatted = employees.stream()
            .map(Employee::getName)
            .collect(Collectors.joining(", ", "[", "]"));

        System.out.println(formatted);
    }
}
```

```text
[Rajesh, Priya, Amit, Kavita, Suresh, Deepak, Neha, Vikram, Ananya, Meera]
```

The `map(Employee::getName)` step converts `Stream<Employee>` to `Stream<String>`. The joining collector inserts ", " between consecutive names and wraps everything in "[" and "]".

### What are the three overloads of joining?

| Overload | Parameters | Example result |
|---|---|---|
| joining() | None | "RajeshPriyaAmit" |
| joining(delimiter) | ", " | "Rajesh, Priya, Amit" |
| joining(delimiter, prefix, suffix) | ", ", "[", "]" | "[Rajesh, Priya, Amit]" |

```java
List<String> names = List.of("Rajesh", "Priya", "Amit");

// No separator
String noSep = names.stream().collect(Collectors.joining());
// "RajeshPriyaAmit"

// Just delimiter
String delim = names.stream().collect(Collectors.joining(", "));
// "Rajesh, Priya, Amit"

// Delimiter + prefix + suffix
String full = names.stream().collect(Collectors.joining(", ", "[", "]"));
// "[Rajesh, Priya, Amit]"
```

### What happens with an empty stream?

```java
String empty = List.<String>of().stream()
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", ", "[", "]"));

System.out.println(empty);  // "[]"
```

For an empty stream, joining returns just prefix + suffix. This produces a clean "[]" instead of a broken fragment.

### How do you handle null elements?

```java
// WRONG: null element throws NullPointerException
try {
    String broken = Stream.of("Rajesh", null, "Priya")
        .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));
} catch (NullPointerException e) {
    System.out.println("Null throws NPE in joining");
}

// CORRECT: filter nulls out
String safe = Stream.of("Rajesh", null, "Priya")
    .filter(Objects::nonNull)
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", ", "[", "]"));
// "[Rajesh, Priya]"

// CORRECT: map nulls to placeholder
String placeholder = Stream.of("Rajesh", null, "Priya")
    .map(n -> n != null ? n : "UNKNOWN")
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", ", "[", "]"));
// "[Rajesh, UNKNOWN, Priya]"
```

### Why is joining more efficient than reduce for string concatenation?

```java
// WRONG: reduce with + creates a new String object at every step
String slow = names.stream()
    .reduce("", (a, b) -> a + ", " + b);
// O(n^2) because each + creates a new String

// CORRECT: joining uses StringBuilder internally
String fast = names.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));
// O(n) because StringBuilder appends in place
```

The reduce approach creates a new String at every concatenation step, giving O(n^2) time complexity. The joining collector uses `StringJoiner` backed by `StringBuilder`, giving O(n) time.

### How do you use joining as a downstream collector?

```java
// Group by department, then join names within each group
Map<String, String> namesByDept = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        Employee::getDepartment,
        Collectors.mapping(
            Employee::getName,
            Collectors.joining(", ", "[", "]"))));

namesByDept.forEach((dept, nameStr) ->
    System.out.println(dept + ": " + nameStr));
```

```text
TRADING: [Rajesh, Priya, Meera]
RISK: [Amit, Kavita, Deepak]
OPERATIONS: [Suresh, Neha]
COMPLIANCE: [Vikram, Ananya]
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake 1: forgetting to map to String before joining.

```java
// WRONG: joining works on CharSequence, not Employee
String wrong = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));  // compile error!

// CORRECT: map to String first
String correct = employees.stream()
    .map(Employee::getName)
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));
```

Mistake 2: getting an extra leading separator with reduce.

```java
// WRONG: reduce gives ", Rajesh, Priya, Amit" (extra leading comma)
String wrong = names.stream()
    .reduce("", (a, b) -> a + ", " + b);

// CORRECT: joining handles the first element correctly
String correct = names.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.joining(", "));
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you join with a different separator for the last element?" There is no built-in support; manually handle the last element or use a third-party library.
- "How do you join with line breaks?" Use `joining("\n")` as the delimiter.
- "How do you limit the joined string to N characters?" Collect first, then truncate with `substring(0, Math.min(n, result.length()))`.
